apter of the Wildlife Society has, to my knowledge, never engaged in advocacy litigation.  We've taken this step because the Forest Service's methodology, which is at issue in this case, is scientifically indefensible.  By relying on inaccurate inventory 
data, the Forest Service invites the over-harvest of rare, ecologically important forest types, and the degradation of important wildlife habitat.  This is inconsistent with our organizational purposes and our Code of Ethics, as quoted above.
\par 
\par 7.\tab The Alaska
 Chapter of The Wildlife Society has long taken an active, leading role in the movement to eliminate the practice of highgrading in the Tongass National Forest.  For example, in 1987 we appealed the Record of Decision for the 1986-90 Operating Period for 
the Alaska Pulp Corporation Long-Term Contract, in large part on the basis of highgrading.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s27\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 8.\tab 
During Congressional deliberations over the Tongass Timber Reform Act of 1990, The Wildlife Society was active in lobbying for the inclusion of section 301(c) (2)
 in the Act.  I testified on this issue in one House committee hearing, and other members testified in other House and Senate hearings. In addition, we submitted extensive written materials in support of this provision to House and Senate members and thei
r staffs.   For several years, both before and after passage of the Act, our members have worked closely on an ongoing basis with Forest Service personnel to discourage highgrading and, since passage of the Act, to implement section 301(c) (2) faithfully.

\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx720\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s27\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 9.\tab 
Members of the Alaska Chapter of the Wildlife Society use and enjoy the Kelp Bay area and other parts of the Tongass for scientific as well as recreational, subsistence, and aesthetic purposes.  These uses are adversely affected by high-grading.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s33\qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f2\fs22 Adverse Effects of Highgrading
\par }\pard\plain \s27\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 10.\tab 
The practice of logging the largest, highest-quality stands of trees first, and leaving the rest behind (hereafter referred to as high-grading) is not new, nor is it unique to southeast Alaska. The most productive timber stands
 in the Pacific Northwest were logged in the first half of this century.   In southeast Alaska, industrial logging over the last 40 years has also concentrated in the higher-volume, more accessible stands. The timber industry has defended high-grading on 
silvicultural or economic grounds, but ecologically, it remains a ruinous practice.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s33\qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx780\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 11.\tab 
There are many different types of old-growth forest in southeast Alaska.  A stand of Sitka spruce, 150 feet tall and 6 feet through, growing on a well-drained riparian 
site, differs markedly from a stand of 30' shore pine, growing on muskeg-like soils near treeline.  Both stands are old growth, but they vary in important ways in terms of their }{\i\f2\fs22 composition}{\f2\fs22 
  (understory and overstory species), their }{\i\f2\fs22 structure}{\f2\fs22  (tree size, spacing, and dead and down wood), and in their }{\i\f2\fs22 function}{\f2\fs22 
 (for example, as wildlife habitat, or in hydrological or nutrient recycling).  Enlightened forest management recognizes and perpetuates this diversity.  In order to maintain a healthy forest ecosystem-- one that continues to }{\i\f2\fs22 function}{
\f2\fs22  well-- essential components of the ecosystem can not be impaired.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx720\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s34\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx780\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 12.\tab 
The importance of forest diversity to wildlife can be illustrated by example of the Sitka black-tailed deer.  Deer utilize a wide variety of forest and 
non-forest habitats during the year, from the beach fringe up to the alpine.  When snow is absent, deer may use open forest stands, including young clearcuts, where forage is relatively abundant.  During most winters, however, an important shift in habita
t
 use occurs.  During periods of deep snow, deer move into high-volume old-growth stands.  There, deer find significantly lower snow depths, and higher forage quality than in open stands. Thus, in the case of deer, a small percentage of the land area is cr
itically important to a large percentage of the population. If that small but critical component of the forest is removed, the effect on deer populations using that landscape is great.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx780\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s27\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 13.\tab Deer, being relatively well studied, provide just one example of wh
y forest diversity is important.  But the argument for maintaining diversity should not rest with deer alone.  There are literally hundreds of species and dozens of ecological processes within the forest that have not been studied.  Maintaining representa
t
ive forest types, in an approximation of their natural size, shape, and spacing, is the only practical approach to maintaining biodiversity in its many forms.  Active management, including logging, can and should occur in this forest. It must be done, how
ever, with careful attention to the first law of "intelligent tinkering," and that is to save all the pieces.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx720\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s37\qc \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx720\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f2\fs22 Lack of Adequate Information
\par }\pard\plain \s34\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx780\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 14.\tab 
Keeping track of those pieces is the responsibility of the U.S. Forest Service.   The Forest Service maintains ma
pped inventory data showing the size, shape, and location of different forest stands across the Tongass.  One of the criteria used to differentiate among old-growth stands is timber volume (number of board feet per acre).  Timber volume varies as a functi
o
n of site productivity (tree size and stocking), species composition, and defect (non-merchantable wood).  The forest in southeast Alaska exhibits a wide range of timber volumes.  Stands on poor sites support less than 8 thousand board feet per acre (volu
m
e class 3), while stands on productive sites have over 50,000 board feet per acre (volume class 7).  The highest volume stands on the Tongass contain up to 125 thousand board feet per acre, and are truly impressive. They are also exceedingly rare. Class 7
 old growth is today found on only 1 percent of the forest.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx780\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s41\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx780\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f2\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {15.\tab 
Despite the economic and ecological importance of relatively rare, high-volume old-growth stands, the Forest Service is unable to identify the location of these stands from the agency's timber
-type inventory maps.  As often as not, stands marked as high volume on the map turn out to really be low volume, while stands marked as low volume on the map turn out to really be high volume.  Using such inaccurate maps to generate reports for Congress,
 
or for the public, is particularly troubling because it effectively validates wrong information.  My personal experience with these maps matches the conclusions of the Forest Service's own reviews:  they can not be used to reliably locate high-volume stan
ds.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx720\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
\par }\pard\plain \s27\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 16.\tab 
Such basic information is essential to sound resource management.  These rare stands are a significant resource economically, ecologically, and aesthetically. The ability to even find these stands for research, education, management, or simple pers
onal enjoyment is largely foreclosed by deficiencies in the current inventory.  Our effort to ensure that reliable resource information  is  available to researchers,  resource managers, Congress,  and  the  general  public  is  consistent  with  the orga
nizational purposes of The Wildlife Society, and is in the public interest.
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\nowidctlpar\tx760\tx1480\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f2\fs22 
